{
  "log": {
    "loglevel": "warning",
    "access": "/var/log/xray/access.log"
  },
  "api": {
    "tag": "api",
    "//": "RoutingService is here, not only in the relay variant, because a node picks its template from one prompt answered before anyone knows what it will be asked to do. CONFIGURE_ROUTE needs AddRule; without it HandlerService.AddOutbound succeeds and the rule that points traffic at that outbound fails with \"unknown service xray.app.router.command.RoutingService\", so the relay half-configures itself and reports nothing wrong. An Xray-entry relay needs nothing else from the relay variant -- the tun bridge there is only for WireGuard/OpenVPN entries -- so carrying this one service makes that whole case work on a node that answered no. It listens on 127.0.0.1:10085 and is inert until called. Cost of being wrong the other way, found 2026-08-13 on the first relay built from this template: a live Iran node that could not carry a single route.",
    "services": ["HandlerService", "RoutingService", "StatsService", "LoggerService"]
  },
  "stats": {},
  "policy": {
    "levels": { "0": { "statsUserUplink": true, "statsUserDownlink": true } },
    "system": { "statsInboundUplink": true, "statsInboundDownlink": true }
  },
  "inbounds": [
    {
      "listen": "127.0.0.1",
      "port": 10085,
      "protocol": "dokodemo-door",
      "settings": { "address": "127.0.0.1" },
      "tag": "api-in"
    },
    {
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": __LISTEN_PORT__,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [],
        "decryption": "none"
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "tcp",
        "security": "reality",
        "realitySettings": {
          "show": false,
          "dest": "__DEST__",
          "xver": 0,
          "serverNames": ["__SERVER_NAME__"],
          "privateKey": "__REALITY_PRIVATE_KEY__",
          "shortIds": ["__SHORT_ID__"]
        }
      },
      "tag": "vless-in"
    },
    {
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": __VLESS_TLS_PORT__,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [],
        "decryption": "none",
        "fallbacks": [
          {
            "//": "The WebSocket carrier, on the same port and the same certificate as this inbound. A path rather than a port of its own is the entire point: a second listener is a second thing to fingerprint and block, while an HTTP upgrade on a normal HTTPS port is what ordinary web applications do all day. Order in this array does not select it -- xray-core keys fallbacks into a [name][alpn][path] map, so a JSON list is unordered here (proxy/vless/inbound/inbound.go). Two properties make this entry win regardless of the h2 entry below: at startup every path registered under the empty ALPN is copied into each named-ALPN bucket that lacks it, and the path itself is found by sniffing the first request line, which does not depend on the negotiated ALPN. That matters because the ALPN really is h2 for these connections -- our clients advertise Chrome's list and this inbound prefers h2 -- so the tempting simplification of dropping either entry breaks the other one.",
            "path": "__VLESS_WS_PATH__",
            "dest": "__VLESS_WS_DEST__",
            "xver": 1
          },
          {
            "//": "Keyed on the negotiated ALPN, and it has to be. This inbound offers h2 so it looks like an ordinary web server, and a browser or any modern HTTP client takes that offer -- then nginx, which only spoke HTTP/1.1, received HTTP/2 frames and the connection died with a protocol error. Measured, not theorised: reproduced from an uncensored machine, and it meant the node API mirrors never worked for an HTTP/2 client. Since nginx before 1.25.1 cannot detect h2c and HTTP/1.1 on one listener -- the http2 flag forces h2c and breaks HTTP/1.1 -- each gets a socket of its own and Xray picks by ALPN.",
            "alpn": "h2",
            "dest": "__TROJAN_FALLBACK_H2__",
            "xver": 1
          },
          {
            "dest": "__TROJAN_FALLBACK__",
            "xver": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "tcp",
        "security": "tls",
        "tlsSettings": {
          "alpn": ["h2", "http/1.1"],
          "certificates": [
            {
              "certificateFile": "__TLS_CERT_PATH__",
              "keyFile": "__TLS_KEY_PATH__"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "tag": "vless-tls-in"
    },
    {
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": __TROJAN_PORT__,
      "protocol": "trojan",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [],
        "fallbacks": [
          {
            "//": "The WebSocket carrier, on the same port and the same certificate as this inbound. A path rather than a port of its own is the entire point: a second listener is a second thing to fingerprint and block, while an HTTP upgrade on a normal HTTPS port is what ordinary web applications do all day. Order in this array does not select it -- xray-core keys fallbacks into a [name][alpn][path] map, so a JSON list is unordered here (proxy/vless/inbound/inbound.go). Two properties make this entry win regardless of the h2 entry below: at startup every path registered under the empty ALPN is copied into each named-ALPN bucket that lacks it, and the path itself is found by sniffing the first request line, which does not depend on the negotiated ALPN. That matters because the ALPN really is h2 for these connections -- our clients advertise Chrome's list and this inbound prefers h2 -- so the tempting simplification of dropping either entry breaks the other one.",
            "path": "__VLESS_WS_PATH__",
            "dest": "__VLESS_WS_DEST__",
            "xver": 1
          },
          {
            "//": "Keyed on the negotiated ALPN, and it has to be. This inbound offers h2 so it looks like an ordinary web server, and a browser or any modern HTTP client takes that offer -- then nginx, which only spoke HTTP/1.1, received HTTP/2 frames and the connection died with a protocol error. Measured, not theorised: reproduced from an uncensored machine, and it meant the node API mirrors never worked for an HTTP/2 client. Since nginx before 1.25.1 cannot detect h2c and HTTP/1.1 on one listener -- the http2 flag forces h2c and breaks HTTP/1.1 -- each gets a socket of its own and Xray picks by ALPN.",
            "alpn": "h2",
            "dest": "__TROJAN_FALLBACK_H2__",
            "xver": 1
          },
          {
            "dest": "__TROJAN_FALLBACK__",
            "xver": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "tcp",
        "security": "tls",
        "tlsSettings": {
          "alpn": ["h2", "http/1.1"],
          "certificates": [
            {
              "certificateFile": "__TLS_CERT_PATH__",
              "keyFile": "__TLS_KEY_PATH__"
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "tag": "trojan-in"
    },
    {
      "//": "Shadowsocks 2022. Its own encryption rather than TLS, so it needs no certificate and shares nothing with the listeners above -- which is the point of offering it: a censor blocking our certificate-presenting ports does not touch this one. On the wire it is indistinguishable from random bytes, so it has no handshake to fingerprint, and equally nothing to hide behind if the port itself is blocked. Only blake3-aes-*-gcm is usable here: xray-core rejects chacha20 for multi-user inbounds, and multi-user is what lets customers be added without a restart.",
      "listen": "0.0.0.0",
      "port": __SHADOWSOCKS_PORT__,
      "protocol": "shadowsocks",
      "settings": {
        "method": "2022-blake3-aes-256-gcm",
        "password": "__SHADOWSOCKS_SERVER_KEY__",
        "//clients": "One seed entry, and it is load-bearing rather than an example. xray-core picks the single-user Shadowsocks server when this array is empty, and that implementation is not a UserManager -- so every add-user RPC is refused with \"proxy is not a UserManager\" and no customer can ever be provisioned. Deploying with an empty array is exactly how that was found. The key is random and never issued to anyone, so the entry authenticates nobody; it exists only to select the multi-user implementation.",
        "clients": [
          {
            "password": "__SHADOWSOCKS_SEED_KEY__",
            "email": "reserved-multiuser-seed"
          }
        ],
        "network": "tcp,udp"
      },
      "tag": "shadowsocks-in"
    },
    {
      "//": "VLESS+TLS carried inside a WebSocket -- the shape Windscribe sells as WStunnel. Loopback-only and with no TLS of its own: the public inbound above already terminated TLS and routed here by request path, so a second handshake would be wrong as well as wasteful. acceptProxyProtocol pairs with that fallback's \"xver\": 1, and without it every customer would appear to arrive from 127.0.0.1 -- which breaks per-user concurrency counting and puts the whole node in one rate-limit bucket.",
      "listen": "127.0.0.1",
      "port": __VLESS_WS_PORT__,
      "protocol": "vless",
      "settings": {
        "clients": [],
        "decryption": "none"
      },
      "streamSettings": {
        "network": "ws",
        "security": "none",
        "wsSettings": {
          "path": "__VLESS_WS_PATH__"
        },
        "sockopt": {
          "acceptProxyProtocol": true
        }
      },
      "tag": "vless-ws-in"
    }
  ],
  "outbounds": [
    { "protocol": "freedom", "tag": "direct" }
  ],
  "routing": {
    "rules": [
      { "type": "field", "inboundTag": ["api-in"], "outboundTag": "api" }
    ]
  }
}
